WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.210
- This is Giant Mine
in the Northwest Territories of Canada.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.400
There are kilometres of tunnels
under this rock,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.460
and chambers the size of city blocks
that have been hollowed out.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.654
In the 20th century, more
than 200,000 tonnes of gold

00:00:11.654 --> 00:00:14.160
were extracted from the
rock underneath here.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.050
At current prices, that is somewhere north
of US $8 billion worth of gold.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.400
There is a problem, though.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.820
That mining also created
more than 200,000 tonnes

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.640
of toxic arsenic trioxide dust.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.830
That's enough to kill everyone
on Earth several times over.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.100
Over the decades, the vast
majority of it was stored

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.415
in the empty, abandoned chambers
left behind after mining,

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:38.800
although some of it did get
out into the environment.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.260
The mine closed in 2004.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.460
When the gold ran out, the
mining company went bankrupt

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.040
and left the mess for the
government to clean up.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.990
Arsenic trioxide has no scent, no taste,
and it dissolves in water.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.220
Oh, and we're right next to Yellowknife,
the capital of the Northwest Territories,

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.010
and next to the 10th
largest lake in the world.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.700
- There's 237,000 tonnes
of arsenic trioxide,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.810
and they're currently stored
in 14 underground chambers.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.090
The plan is to freeze the chambers.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.250
We're freezing the rock
around the arsenic.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.233
See, arsenic trioxide is a fine powder.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:12.840
So it will form a shell.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.160
And eventually, it will freeze in
and it will become a block over time.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.490
And that prevents water from
getting in and getting out

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.000
and keeps the arsenic trioxide contained.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.410
So each chamber, of course,
is differently shaped.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.370
Some are regular shaped.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.070
Some are very rectangular shaped.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.820
So we want to freeze 10 metres
beyond all boundaries of the chambers,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.700
both on the horizontal
and the vertical.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.150
We're going to freeze
at least to -5°C.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.130
The way we're going to
freeze it, we're going to use

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.610
a technology called
thermosiphon technology,

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.980
and it's basically a technology that uses

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.511
the ambient air temperature, the cold air,

00:01:45.511 --> 00:01:49.147
using a long tube filed
with carbon dioxide.

00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:52.396
The carbon dioxide, when it's a gas,
it rises up to the top of the tube.

00:01:52.396 --> 00:01:54.800
And then if the air outside is colder,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.370
it will dissipate its heat,
turn to a liquid,

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.800
and with gravity, will drop back
down to the bottom of the tube.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.700
And there, it can warm up, take
the heat out of the ground,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.950
and it just continually cycles,
extracting heat from the ground.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.360
Since we're in Yellowknife,
north of 60° latitude,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.350
it's colder above ground
than it is below ground

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.980
for the majority of the year.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.940
- Freezing the arsenic
doesn't make it less toxic,

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.700
but it does make it
impossible for any water

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.060
to seep in and then carry the arsenic out.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.610
And that was the mining
company's original solution

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.466
using the natural permafrost here.

00:02:23.466 --> 00:02:26.500
And it might have worked, if they
hadn't mined the rock above it,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.680
exposed the permafrost
layer to the elements,

00:02:28.691 --> 00:02:30.960
and started to melt it.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.170
Freezing should work,

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.190
but there are skeptics,
and they do have a point.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.730
Freezing requires constant upkeep.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.550
This dust is arguably worse
than radioactive waste.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.820
There is no half-life here.
It won't decay over time.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:47.510
The dust will be just as deadly
in 10,000, 100,000 or a million years.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.281
- So right now, there's
nothing on the horizon.

00:02:50.281 --> 00:02:54.590
The freezing was deemed the best
technology for the arsenic trioxide.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.530
So we will be funding
research indefinitely

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.220
until a different solution arises.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.710
We have frozen one of the chambers.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.410
We did what we call a
freeze optimization study.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:07.670
We chose one of the chambers.
We installed the thermosiphons,

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.760
but we also installed some
freezing pipes filled with glycol

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.310
to do what we call an active freeze,

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.053
which will be very
similar to a hockey rink.

00:03:15.053 --> 00:03:18.500
So based on that study, we
determined that we could use

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.240
the passive technology, which
is just the thermosiphons

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.690
with the carbon dioxide.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.500
It just wasn't quite as fast,
so it's gonna take a little bit longer,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.180
but the trade-offs are such
that we don't need to use power

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.960
and it's a lower maintenance
over the longer term.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.560
So the remaining chambers will be frozen
when we start the remediation project.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.570
It could be as early as summer of 2020,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.370
but more optimistically, 2021.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.730
Our last estimate for
the cost of the project

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.810
was 934 million, including
the care and maintenance phase

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.550
that we're in right now,
remediation that we'll go into

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.520
in a few years, and then the
long term care and maintenance.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.380
This project has gotten
approval for 100 years.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:56.060
- These days, countries
in the developed world

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.260
have regulations that
mean mining companies

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.060
shouldn't be able to do this.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.170
We have environmental impact
studies and long-term plans,

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.366
and we have public scrutiny
of pretty much everything.

00:04:06.366 --> 00:04:09.012
But we still have to fix
the mistakes of the past

00:04:09.012 --> 00:04:11.220
and make sure that
today's mining companies

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.600
can't just walk away from their legacies.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.850
Thanks to everyone at the
Giant Mine Remediation Project.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:18.740
Pull down the description
for links about them

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:21.570
and about some of the history
that I couldn't fit into this video.

